course: biology
lesson: 175
title: The twilight zone: barreleyes, lanternfish and the biggest daily journey on Earth
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Dive down and the light fades like evening: below about two hundred metres it is a dim blue twilight, and below about a thousand metres sunlight is gone completely.
explain: In this twilight zone many animals make their own light, with rows of tiny lamps on their bellies, glowing clouds they squirt at enemies, or flashes like a torch switched on and off.
explain: Every night, when it is dark above, huge crowds of fish, shrimp and squid swim up hundreds of metres to feed near the surface and sink back down at dawn, so many that ship sonar (which measures depth by sending sound down and timing the echo) once mistook them for the sea floor rising.
explain: Think of it as the whole town getting in a lift every evening to eat on the top floor and riding back down before sunrise: it is the biggest journey animals make anywhere on Earth, every single day.
fact: Sunlight gets weaker as you go deeper; below about two hundred metres it is very dim, and below about a thousand metres there is no sunlight at all.
fact: The barreleye fish has a clear dome on its head, and its green eyes look up through it; its see-through head was only filmed alive in 2009, because nets used to destroy it.
fact: Lanternfish have light organs called photophores along their bodies, and they are among the most common fish on Earth; only the tiny bristlemouths may be more numerous.
fact: Huge numbers of animals swim up from the deep at night to feed near the surface and go back down at dawn, and this daily migration is the biggest movement of animals on Earth.
fact: The animals rise only at night because darkness hides them from hunters that find food by sight near the surface.
fact: Lights on the belly match the faint light coming from above, so a hunter looking up from below cannot see the animal's dark shape.
fact: Hatchetfish have thin silver bodies, lights on their bellies, and eyes that point upwards to spot food falling from above.
fact: Comb jellies have rows of tiny beating hairs that break light into rainbow colours; they are not true jellyfish and have no stinging cells.
fact: Some deep-sea shrimp squirt a glowing cloud to confuse a hunter, a little like squid ink but made of light.
fact: Many deep-sea animals are red or black, because red light does not reach the deep, so a red animal looks black and is hard to see; some deep fish even make red light that most other animals cannot see.
fact: A siphonophore looks like one long jellyfish but is a chain of many tiny animals living together, and some are longer than a blue whale.
fact: The fangtooth has some of the biggest teeth for its size of any fish, so long that it has pockets in its head to fit them when it closes its mouth.
fact: The cockeyed squid has one big eye that looks up and one small eye that looks down, and it swims tilted to keep each eye pointing the right way.
exercise: Below about how many metres is the sea very dim? | About two hundred metres | Only about two metres down | Only about twenty metres down | About two thousand kilometres
exercise: Below about how many metres is there no sunlight at all? | About a thousand metres | Only about ten metres down | Only about a hundred metres | There is always some sunlight
exercise: What is special about the barreleye fish? | It has a clear dome on its head with eyes looking up through it | It has eyes on the end of its long tail | It has no head at all | It lives in an old wooden barrel that fell off a ship and sank
exercise: Why was the barreleye's see-through head only filmed alive in 2009? | Nets used to destroy it when the fish was caught | It only grew a head in 2009, after millions of years | Nobody had cameras before | It hid inside barrels
exercise: What are photophores? | Light organs on the bodies of fish like lanternfish | Photos taken by divers with flash cameras deep down | Tiny fins for steering | Gills for breathing
exercise: Which fish may be even more numerous than lanternfish? | Bristlemouths | Giant whale sharks | Common goldfish | Great white sharks
exercise: What happens in the twilight zone every night? | Huge numbers of animals swim up to feed and go back down at dawn | All the animals swim down and fall asleep on the dark sea floor till dawn | The water freezes for the night | The animals swim to the beach
exercise: Why do a hatchetfish's eyes point upwards? | To spot food falling from above | To watch the stars at night | Because it always swims upside down | To look for the sea floor
exercise: What makes a comb jelly flash rainbow colours? | Rows of tiny beating hairs that break up light | Rainbow paint all over its soft see-through body | Electric shocks | Lightning from the surface
exercise: Can a comb jelly sting you? | No, it has no stinging cells | Yes, it stings worse than any jellyfish | Yes, but only at night | Only if it is rainbow coloured
exercise: Why does a deep-sea shrimp spit glowing liquid? | To confuse a hunter | To see where it is going | To cook its food with heat | To call its babies home
exercise: Why are many deep-sea animals red? | Red light does not reach the deep, so red looks black there | Red keeps them warm | They blush in the cold | They eat red food only, like shrimp, and the colour stays in them for life
exercise: What is a siphonophore? | A chain of many tiny animals living together | One giant jellyfish with a very long tail | A kind of sea snake | A long piece of seaweed
exercise: How long can some siphonophores be? | Longer than a blue whale | About as long as a pencil | About as long as your arm | As long as a goldfish in a bowl
exercise: How does the fangtooth close its mouth over its long teeth? | It has pockets in its head to fit them | It cannot close its mouth at all, ever | It pulls its teeth out at night | Its teeth bend like rubber
exercise: Why does the cockeyed squid have two different eyes? | The big eye looks up and the small eye looks down | One eye is broken | It is only a baby | The small eye is for smelling, and the big one for tasting
test: «sonar says the sea floor came up 300 metres at night wtf» What is really happening? | Huge crowds of animals are swimming up to feed in the dark | The sea floor really moves up every night and back down by day | The sonar is always broken at night | The sea is getting shallower forever
test: Why do so many animals swim up only at night and not by day? | In the dark it is harder for hunters near the surface to see them | Sunlight burns them in seconds | The surface water is colder by day | They cannot swim during the day, so they lie still on the bottom till dark
test: «3 cockeyed squids, how many eyes altogether, and how many big ones» | 6 eyes, 3 big | 3 eyes, 3 big | 6 eyes, 6 big | 4 eyes, 2 big
test: A red shrimp is lit by a white lamp on a submarine and looks bright red. Why is it hard to see in its own home? | No red light reaches the deep, so it looks black there | It turns blue when the lamp goes off | Fish there cannot see at all | It lives inside rocks
test: «that rainbow jellyfish thing is gonna sting me, run» What is right? | It is a comb jelly, which has no stinging cells | Yes, rainbow colours always mean it is very poisonous | Yes, it shocks with electricity | It is a true jellyfish like all the others
test: In a cartoon a deep-sea fish switches on a torch held in its fin. What is the real version? | Many deep animals make their own light with light organs | Deep fish really carry little torches | Deep fish use fire to see | No animal can make its own light, it is all reflected from the moon
test: What would happen if a lanternfish turned off all its belly lights? | A hunter below might see its dark shape against the faint light above | It would glow even more | It would sink to the bottom | Nothing, the lights are only for fun
test: «my mate says that long jelly thing is one animal» What is right? | A siphonophore is a chain of many tiny animals living together | Yes, it is one huge jellyfish with a very long tail, like a kite | It is a sea snake | It is a piece of rope
test: «barreleye pics always looked rubbish, why» What is the reason? | Nets destroyed its see-through head until it was filmed alive in 2009 | It only lives in fish tanks | It has no head | It is too fast to film
test: A diver goes down and down and the water turns from blue to dark. At about what depth is there no sunlight at all? | About a thousand metres | Only about ten metres or so | Only about fifty metres down | About ten thousand metres
test: «fangtooth can't shut its mouth, poor thing» What is right? | It can, because pockets in its head fit the long teeth | Right, it never closes it | It has no teeth at all | It closes it by pulling out its teeth one by one and putting them back later
exam: Identify the phenomenon which is acknowledged to constitute the largest movement of animals upon Earth. | The nightly ascent and dawn descent of animals of the twilight zone | The seasonal migration of wildebeest | The annual flight of swallows to Africa | The migration of salmon up rivers
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the comb jelly? | It produces rainbow colours by means of beating hairs and possesses no stinging cells | It is a true jellyfish of exceptional venom | It emits electric discharges to stun its prey | It is a species of fish with transparent skin
exam: Ascertain the reason for which numerous animals of the deep sea are of red colouration. | Red light does not penetrate to such depths, so that red bodies appear black | The red pigment retains heat in cold water | Red colouration attracts prey from the surface | Such animals consume exclusively red food
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the siphonophore. | It is a colonial organism composed of many small animals, some exceeding a blue whale in length | It is a single jellyfish of modest dimensions | It is a species of marine plant | It is a variety of deep-sea eel
